00:31:50.720 --> 00:31:55.919 align:start position:19%
going to cluster them in this way or
whether they're going to uh the clusters

00:31:54.159 --> 00:32:00.960 align:start position:19%
will will look slightly different in the
world. And that's part of uh part of the

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:07.440 align:start position:19%
work that we're doing now. Um, but
you'll notice these uh across the top

00:32:03.440 --> 00:32:13.200 align:start position:19%
row in each box are letters and these
represent the uh the different measures

00:32:10.559 --> 00:32:18.559 align:start position:19%
that exist for for assessing uh each
each possible adverse event type and the

00:32:15.760 --> 00:32:23.360 align:start position:19%
X's uh represent whether that measure
actually captures uh or is designed to

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:28.320 align:start position:19%
capture that adverse event. So that's
that's what you're seeing with the grids

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:33.120 align:start position:19%
and the X's. Uh something that I want to
point out that I think stood out to us

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:39.600 align:start position:19%
right away is that the instrument that
had the most coverage of any uh of all

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:44.399 align:start position:19%
of the adverse event criteria that we
were looking at was actually an

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:50.559 align:start position:19%
instrument that was designed to capture
adverse events occurring in um

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:56.399 align:start position:19%
meditationbased uh interventions. And I
think that that reflects some of what

00:32:53.039 --> 00:33:01.120 align:start position:19%
we're seeing is that the impacts of uh
of psychedelics can be uh can be broader

00:32:59.440 --> 00:33:05.840 align:start position:19%
than just physiological impact. They
they impact more than just mood, more

00:33:03.039 --> 00:33:10.480 align:start position:19%
than just um uh these sort of
psychiatric endpoints, but they can

00:33:07.919 --> 00:33:15.760 align:start position:19%
reach into uh metacognitive,
existential, and even spiritual domains.

00:33:13.760 --> 00:33:19.600 align:start position:19%
And so it's important to assess the
relevant criteria.

00:33:17.840 --> 00:33:24.080 align:start position:19%
I'll just talk about a few of these uh
for the interest of time. Um and the

00:33:22.640 --> 00:33:30.000 align:start position:19%
first one that I'll mention is the
psychosspiritual domain. Um this is a

00:33:26.799 --> 00:33:32.799 align:start position:19%
formulation that I think uh people
who've spent some time with the

00:33:31.279 --> 00:33:35.600 align:start position:19%
psychedelic research world will probably
be familiar with that there's a sort of

00:33:34.559 --> 00:33:40.720 align:start position:19%
there are these spiritual and
existential impacts and there is sort of

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:45.679 align:start position:19%
an open question about the extent to
which these uh these spiritual or

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:51.919 align:start position:19%
existential effects are actually driving
some of the therapeutic benefits. Um the

00:33:50.559 --> 00:33:57.679 align:start position:19%
basically the short version of the
answer is we don't have enough data to

00:33:53.360 --> 00:34:02.399 align:start position:19%
know yet. But what I became interested
in along with my colleagues

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:07.360 align:start position:19%
we we know that if there are benefits to
spirituality people tend to do better.

00:34:04.159 --> 00:34:13.679 align:start position:19%
But what happens if uh people experience
spiritual difficulties? So if somebody

00:34:09.440 --> 00:34:18.159 align:start position:19%
takes uh psilocybin uh and you know
they're expecting to have this this

00:34:15.919 --> 00:34:23.599 align:start position:19%
beautiful spiritual experience they're
uh to to to meet their creator and

00:34:21.919 --> 00:34:27.679 align:start position:19%
instead what they experience is really
just a whole lot of nothing or worse,

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:32.000 align:start position:19%
right? And they come away with it
profoundly from it profoundly

00:34:29.440 --> 00:34:37.599 align:start position:19%
disappointed or uh have a crisis of
faith. um that is that's going to be an

00:34:35.839 --> 00:34:42.879 align:start position:19%
added difficulty that they're now
dealing with after the psilocybin

00:34:40.079 --> 00:34:47.599 align:start position:19%
treatment and uh we've we've been
looking at this in a couple of uh

00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:52.399 align:start position:19%
different studies and papers as well as
uh as colleagues around the world. Um,

00:34:50.240 --> 00:34:57.119 align:start position:19%
if you're interested in this, I really
encourage folks to find Rachel

00:34:54.320 --> 00:35:00.960 align:start position:19%
Peterson's essay, um, a theological
reckoning with bad trips, which was part

00:34:59.359 --> 00:35:06.720 align:start position:19%
published in the Harvard Divinity
Bulletin, uh, where she describes her

00:35:03.119 --> 00:35:11.680 align:start position:19%
own experience of, uh, of difficulty uh,
after taking psilocybin as part of a

00:35:08.960 --> 00:35:15.839 align:start position:19%
clinical trial.
So the kinds of domains that we're

00:35:13.680 --> 00:35:23.520 align:start position:19%
looking at here include existential
distress um death anxiety which is not

00:35:19.200 --> 00:35:29.040 align:start position:19%
um like being afraid that one is in
imminent danger but distress about being

00:35:25.839 --> 00:35:33.920 align:start position:19%
mortal um spiritual struggles within
oneself but also spiritual struggles

00:35:31.119 --> 00:35:38.400 align:start position:19%
with one's community of faith. If you
are part of a religious community and

00:35:36.560 --> 00:35:43.599 align:start position:19%
you rely on that for your well-being,
for your social capital, for your uh

00:35:41.520 --> 00:35:48.160 align:start position:19%
community and suddenly there's a rift
with that community after a psychedelic

00:35:45.680 --> 00:35:53.599 align:start position:19%
treatment that could undermine a
person's resilience and we want to be

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:58.079 align:start position:19%
able to uh to monitor for that. Uh, of
course, religious or spiritual abuse or

00:35:56.079 --> 00:36:02.079 align:start position:19%
imposition are important to monitor and
unfortunately we know that these happen

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:08.480 align:start position:19%
although more often in the sort of in
the gray or underground space. Um,

00:36:06.160 --> 00:36:14.720 align:start position:19%
the other set of adverse effects I want
to talk about are psychotherapy related

00:36:10.640 --> 00:36:20.400 align:start position:19%
harms. Uh there was a wonderful uh essay
published about 3 years ago about how um

00:36:18.800 --> 00:36:26.240 align:start position:19%
studying harms is key to improving
psychedelic therapies. And what the

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:30.960 align:start position:19%
authors say is it would be unimaginable
for clinical trials using a combination

00:36:28.160 --> 00:36:35.119 align:start position:19%
of two drugs to only examine or regulate
one of the two components. However, this

00:36:33.359 --> 00:36:39.760 align:start position:19%
is the norm in psychedelic research in
which the psychological support is

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:43.839 align:start position:19%
neither systematized nor evaluated as in
traditional psychotherapy research and I

00:36:42.320 --> 00:36:50.000 align:start position:19%
don't think we're actually doing a very
good job evaluating it in traditional

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:55.520 align:start position:19%
psychotherapy research but uh but she
they do have a point um so adverse we

00:36:53.760 --> 00:37:00.480 align:start position:19%
know that adverse event assessment that
relies only on pharmarmacologically

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:04.000 align:start position:19%
derived criteria
underestimates adverse effects related

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:09.520 align:start position:19%
to behavioral, lifestyle, and
psychological therapies uh routinely.

00:37:07.119 --> 00:37:14.800 align:start position:19%
And we also know that adverse effects do
occur at regular rates in everyday

00:37:12.640 --> 00:37:20.160 align:start position:19%
run-of-the-mill psychotherapies. So
about 3 to 7% of patients in uh standard

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:25.920 align:start position:19%
psychotherapies experience adverse
effects and 7 to 15% in treatments for

00:37:23.680 --> 00:37:30.320 align:start position:19%
substance use disorders.
And if we're uh and this is important to

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:36.720 align:start position:19%
note because psychedelics are also used
for substance use disorders sometimes.

00:37:33.440 --> 00:37:40.880 align:start position:19%
Um so the kinds of adverse effects we're
monitoring are uh strain in the

00:37:39.040 --> 00:37:44.720 align:start position:19%
therapeutic relationship, excessive
dependence on the therapist, which

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:51.599 align:start position:19%
unfortunately we've seen happen with
psychedelics. Um uh therapist abuse or

00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:56.720 align:start position:19%
exploitation, feelings of abandonment,
uh of course any kind of a boundary

00:37:53.520 --> 00:38:00.880 align:start position:19%
violation or transgression. And this is
uh this is actually something that that

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:04.160 align:start position:19%
came up in a conversation with Rob, but
uh loss of confidence toward

00:38:02.480 --> 00:38:08.640 align:start position:19%
interventions. I think this is something
that's especially important to watch for

00:38:05.680 --> 00:38:13.599 align:start position:19%
insight in insilocybin therapies when
these can be thought of by patients as a

00:38:11.440 --> 00:38:18.880 align:start position:19%
treatment of last resort sometimes that
this is the you know I've tried I've

00:38:16.320 --> 00:38:24.240 align:start position:19%
tried Prozac I've tried psychotherapy
I've tried all these things nothing has

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:28.560 align:start position:19%
helped me but I think psilocybin can and
if that person comes away without

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:33.359 align:start position:19%
improvement is that person coming away
with a sense that nothing can help them

00:38:30.880 --> 00:38:37.680 align:start position:19%
now so we this is something that we want
to avoid. And this is something that

00:38:35.440 --> 00:38:42.000 align:start position:19%
every team that I know that has been
doing psilocybin research has worked

00:38:39.440 --> 00:38:46.079 align:start position:19%
very hard to avoid that framing. And yet
they tell me, you know, it's really hard

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:50.119 align:start position:19%
to hard to escape that a patient might
have that view. Anyway,

00:38:51.119 --> 00:38:57.760 align:start position:19%
so the last domain that I want to
mention and I think this might be of

00:38:55.119 --> 00:39:03.599 align:start position:19%
particular interest to this group is uh
socioultural uh harms, socioultural

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:08.320 align:start position:19%
adverse events. So um generally speaking
and I'm I'm relying on some work by by

00:39:05.599 --> 00:39:14.400 align:start position:19%
Dennis Went uh Joseph Gan and Donna
Nagata um that primarily they work with

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:20.320 align:start position:19%
First Nations and Native American
communities and they talk about you know

00:39:16.160 --> 00:39:24.480 align:start position:19%
how do you uh monitor for cultural harms
uh in these communities and first they

00:39:22.640 --> 00:39:32.079 align:start position:19%
notice well you know we actually don't
have any evidence uh of harms there's no

00:39:28.240 --> 00:39:36.640 align:start position:19%
denial of care or or early dropout um no
uh increase in intensive care, no

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:40.000 align:start position:19%
critical misdiagnosis that we're seeing,
but the absence of evidence doesn't mean

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:42.720 align:start position:19%
an evidence of absence. And largely,
it's the same story that I've mentioned

00:39:41.440 --> 00:39:46.400 align:start position:19%
with psychedelics. We're just not
assessing any of these things. There's

00:39:44.400 --> 00:39:50.640 align:start position:19%
lack of assessment or research. There's
lack of any frameworks in psychiatry for

00:39:48.560 --> 00:39:55.680 align:start position:19%
assessing these uh negative cultural
impacts. And there is an absence of any

00:39:53.920 --> 00:40:01.440 align:start position:19%
uh and yet there is long-standing
evidence that these harms do occur. So

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:05.440 align:start position:19%
if you haven't read these books, I
really recommend them. They're not easy

00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:11.520 align:start position:19%
reads. Uh medical apartheid, uh the
protest, psychosis, bad medicine, um are

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:17.200 align:start position:19%
all important just to sort of uh
familiarize ourselves with the ongoing

00:40:13.680 --> 00:40:21.599 align:start position:19%
and certainly historic uh cultural harms
that can be perpetrated in uh in

00:40:19.680 --> 00:40:27.920 align:start position:19%
standard therapies.
Um and we do have some emerging evidence

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:34.079 align:start position:19%
in uh the psychedelic research that
patients and stakeholders have reported

00:40:30.240 --> 00:40:37.200 align:start position:19%
cultural harms. So I I think it's it's
probably inappropriate to make this

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:42.160 align:start position:19%
connection of absence of evidence to
evidence of absence and instead we can

00:40:39.920 --> 00:40:47.119 align:start position:19%
recognize that harm itself is a social
